lesson('large-group-activity-zoning','Design a large-group rental around simultaneous activities',
'Plan a group property for cooking, meals, conversation, quiet time and sleep at the same time, with a usable activity map and room tests.',[
('Start with simultaneous use, rather than the bed count',
'''A group rental is a collection of overlapping activities. At breakfast, some guests are cooking, others are seated, one person may be working and another may still be sleeping. A plan that works only when everyone behaves the same way will reveal its limitations after arrival. Begin with the activities you intend to support and the rooms where they can happen together.

Use a simple time-of-day map. List arrival, breakfast, daytime departures, return from activities, dinner, evening conversation and bedtime. Under each, write where bags, shoes, food, equipment and people will be. Record conflicts rather than solving them immediately. This distinguishes a missing furniture item from a zoning problem that another chair cannot resolve. Keep the planned guest group separate from any legal occupancy determination.'''),
('Give gathering spaces a clear job',
'''Identify the principal gathering area and count actual usable seats in its normal configuration. Include the distance and orientation between seats, the ability to reach a table and the route through the room. A stool in a corridor is not equivalent to a comfortable seat in the conversation zone. Test the arrangement with people occupying it rather than inspecting empty furniture footprints.

Where the property allows it, give a second space a different function: quiet reading, a smaller conversation, a work surface or an activity table. Avoid furnishing two rooms with the same arrangement merely because a matching package is available. The second room can absorb an activity that otherwise disrupts the main space. State the intended function in the brief so its lighting, outlets and storage follow the same plan.'''),
('Test meals at the promised scale',
'''Seat the intended group and simulate serving a meal. Open the refrigerator, dishwasher and cabinets while someone is seated nearby. Pull chairs out and walk the principal route. If an extended table is part of the plan, test the extended configuration and record where the spare chairs and leaves live. A capacity claim should describe a configuration that the local team can actually prepare.

An outdoor table can complement indoor dining but introduces weather, lighting and maintenance dependencies. Decide how the property functions when outdoor space is unavailable. The right response may involve rearrangement, a different furnishing choice or a clearer description of the experience. Avoid silently assuming that every group will eat outside or split across rooms.'''),
('Protect quieter uses and private routes',
'''Mark the path from bedrooms to bathrooms and the entry. Check whether guests must cross the main seating area to reach another essential room. Place late-night activities thoughtfully, accounting for adjacent bedrooms, shared walls and the actual building. Soft furnishings may change how a room feels, but they do not establish a sound-isolation rating or solve every source of noise.

Test the property after dark. Open and close doors, turn on the ordinary lights and use the kitchen at a normal level. Record observations without claiming a technical acoustic measurement. If noise transfer is a material concern, obtain appropriate assessment before selecting construction changes. Use the observations to adjust activity placement and guest instructions.'''),
('Make the plan repeatable for the local team',
'''Photograph each accepted configuration with the furniture in its actual position. Record which table arrangement is standard, where spare seating is stored and which routes must remain clear. The turnover team needs an attainable reset, not an idealized photograph that requires moving every heavy item before each stay.

At the first operating review, collect repeated questions and observed congestion. Link each issue to the original activity map, then choose a specific remedy and a test. Do not interpret a booking change as proof that zoning caused it. The immediate measure is whether the property performs its stated activities more coherently.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a ten-person house has a living room with six comfortable seats and a second room used mainly for decoration. The kitchen island blocks the path to the rear door when stools are occupied. The owner gives the second room a quiet gathering function, tests a revised island seating arrangement and checks the dinner setup with ten place settings. The solution follows observed activities, rather than adding four loose chairs wherever they fit.''',
['Map at least three overlapping activities during breakfast and evening use.','Test actual seated configurations, door openings and serving routes.','Record the indoor plan when outdoor dining is unavailable.','Walk bedroom-to-bathroom routes while common rooms are occupied.','Give the cleaner photographs and storage locations for every approved configuration.'],
['Time of day','Simultaneous activities','Room and usable configuration','Observed conflict','Proposed change','Retest result and reviewer'],
['/design-roadmap/guest-and-property-brief/','/resources/layouts/'],['amenities'])
lesson('studio-rental-zoning-without-extra-furniture','Zone a studio rental without filling every corner',
'Use a measured activity sequence to coordinate sleeping, luggage, dining, working and circulation in a compact studio.',[
('Describe the stay before choosing multifunction furniture',
'''A small rental has little capacity to absorb an unnecessary piece. Write a short sequence of how guests arrive, unpack, prepare food, sit, sleep and leave. Identify which activities can share a surface at different times and which need to occur simultaneously. A desk used for dining may be acceptable for one brief but unsuitable when two guests need a work call and a meal at the same time.

List fixed constraints before movable furnishings: the kitchenette, bathroom door, windows, radiators, outlets, built-in storage and entry. Record actual clear dimensions and door swings. Distinguish required functions from attractive extras. An empty corner may be needed for a suitcase, cleaning access or a turning movement; it is not automatically a decorating opportunity.'''),
('Compare the ordinary and expanded configurations',
'''Convertible furniture creates more than one room plan. Draw the sofa bed open and closed, the folding table in use and stored, and the chair pulled away from its surface. Include the bedding and moving parts. Check that the bathroom and exit routes remain usable during sleeping, not only during photography.

Perform a complete setup with the people who will manage the property. Record how long it takes, where bedding is stored and whether heavy pieces must move. Follow the exact product instructions and refer uncertainty about safety, access or building requirements to the responsible professional. A compact product photograph does not establish that its mechanism can be used comfortably in the available room.'''),
('Use storage to remove real friction',
'''Assign locations to luggage, coats, shoes, toiletries, groceries and charging. Separate guest storage from the owner's operating stock. Test a suitcase in the proposed location with drawers and doors open. Hooks and shelves should follow suitable installation requirements for the actual wall and product; do not improvise mounting based on appearance.

A storage bench is useful only if its access works with the room occupied. Check whether a seated guest must move before another guest can reach a bag. A high shelf may provide storage but fail the intended guest brief. Describe the installed feature accurately and let the actual operating test guide the specification.'''),
('Create visual order with fewer deliberate decisions',
'''Coordinate large fixed and movable elements before adding accessories. Compare finishes, scale and the view from the entry and bed. A repeated color or material can create a coherent relationship, while several unrelated small pieces may make the room feel busier without adding a function. Test samples in the actual light instead of relying on a universal rule about light or dark colors.

Keep lighting controls and power access understandable from the guest's usual positions. Avoid extending cords through walking routes to compensate for furniture placement. Electrical work belongs with qualified professionals. Where possible, choose a layout that uses the installed service points appropriately rather than treating them as a styling problem to conceal.'''),
('Document what guests will actually receive',
'''Photograph the sleeping configuration as well as the daytime arrangement if the conversion materially changes the room. Show relationships between zones. State what the dining or work surface is and which furniture converts. A clear description helps guests assess suitability without a broad claim that the space works for every traveler.

Give the manager a setup and reset record. After early stays, review repeated questions about storage, bed conversion and controls. Test a proposed change before buying another piece. The goal is a complete, usable compact arrangement, not the highest possible object count.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a studio has a sofa bed, a small desk and two side tables. Opening the bed prevents the desk chair from moving and leaves no luggage location. The owner tests removing one table and revising the desk orientation, then completes the sleeping setup with two suitcases present. The published photos show the accepted sleeping arrangement and identify the shared dining/work surface.''',
['Draw all moving and convertible items in use.','Test the room with the intended luggage and guest count.','Record where bedding, bags and owner stock live.','Check controls and outlets from each occupied position.','Photograph meaningful daytime and nighttime configurations.'],
['Activity','Normal furniture position','Expanded position','Shared surface or storage','Conflict observed','Accepted arrangement'],
['/design-roadmap/measured-layout-and-delivery-access/','/resources/layouts/'],['amenities','routes'])
lesson('multigenerational-stay-design-brief','Write a design brief for multigenerational stays',
'Coordinate different sleep schedules, gathering preferences, routes, storage and clear property descriptions without assuming every family has the same needs.',[
('Define activities and choices without stereotyping',
'''Multigenerational describes a group composition, not a uniform ability or preference. Some guests want quiet mornings, some need a work surface, and some prefer to stay close to the main gathering area. Ask which choices the property can provide rather than assigning a predictable behavior to a guest's age.

Start with bedroom distribution, bathrooms, stairs and common rooms. Identify what is fixed and what a furnishing plan can change. Keep the design brief specific: a reading place away from the television, a room with a shorter bathroom route or storage that does not require using a high shelf. Avoid describing a property as universally accessible because a few individual features are convenient.'''),
('Give guests more than one way to gather',
'''Test the main seating arrangement for conversation, viewing and movement. Where space allows, add a secondary activity zone with a distinct role. A smaller table or a quiet seating area can let different activities happen together without asking everyone to occupy one room. It should still have the lighting and surfaces needed for its actual function.

Compare the routes between these zones. A quiet room loses some usefulness if everyone must cross it to reach the kitchen. A comfortable chair loses usefulness when luggage blocks its approach. Use a simple floor plan and an ordinary-use walkthrough to find these conflicts before selecting more furnishings.'''),
('Review bedroom and bathroom relationships',
'''List each bedroom's actual bed configuration, bathroom access and relevant changes in level. Test ordinary nighttime use with the actual lights and doors. Review where guests place a phone, glasses, luggage and clothing. Individual preferences differ, so describe the available arrangement rather than promising a universal sleep outcome.

When considering specialized support products, use the correct product instructions and qualified advice. A decorative shelf or towel bar should not be represented as a support device. A room photograph cannot establish the suitability of a fixture for a particular guest. The owner's role is to document the installed features accurately and respond to specific questions with verified information.'''),
('Make storage and controls easy to understand',
'''Use consistent locations and straightforward labels where needed. Keep guest essentials visible enough to find without exposing the entire owner's supply inventory. Test the property with somebody who has not seen the plan. Ask that person to find bedding, operate the ordinary lights and locate the kitchen items required for a meal.

Record confusing controls and revise the instruction or arrangement. Do not compensate for an inaccessible or faulty installation with increasingly elaborate instructions. If a task needs specialist work, assign it to the appropriate person. Keep the final guide aligned with what is actually installed.'''),
('Use property evidence in the listing',
'''Airbnb's accessibility guidance emphasizes specific feature information and supporting photographs. Apply that principle by recording the entrance, routes, rooms and bathroom features as they exist. Use dimensions and photographs to answer questions where appropriate, and distinguish a verified feature from an unresolved assumption.

Review the description after a furnishing change. Moving a large cabinet may affect a route shown in older images. Keep feature evidence dated and tell the listing owner which revision applies. A well-prepared description allows guests to evaluate their own needs without relying on a vague claim about suitability for every family.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a family-focused rental has a ground-floor bedroom but its bathroom is upstairs. The owner records that relationship clearly, rather than calling the bedroom an accessible suite. A quieter sitting area is furnished away from the television, and the room guide identifies the actual routes and controls. Guest questions can then be answered using the current plan and photographs.''',
['Describe options and constraints without age-based assumptions.','Test overlapping quiet and social activities.','Record bedroom, bathroom and stair relationships.','Check unfamiliar-user control and storage tasks.','Keep listing feature photographs current.'],
['Guest activity or question','Available room or feature','Verified fact','Photograph or measurement','Open question','Listing or guide update'],
['/design-roadmap/guest-and-property-brief/','/resources/comfort/'],['accessibility','routes'])
lesson('pet-friendly-furnishing-system','Specify a pet-friendly rental as a care system',
'Coordinate approved pet policies, washable components, cleaning methods, storage and replacement records instead of choosing furniture from a single durability label.',[
('Translate the operating policy into design requirements',
'''Begin with the property's actual pet policy and local operating process. Identify which rooms and furnishings guests are permitted to use with pets, where supplies are stored and who answers questions. A pet-friendly label does not determine the cleaning method, insurance terms or permitted use of a particular product.

Walk the arrival and departure sequence. Consider where guests place leads, wet gear, towels and waste supplies, without assuming any feature eliminates damage or odor. Keep the policy separate from the product specification. The designer needs to know the activities and expected exposure; the manager needs a clear process that can be communicated and maintained.'''),
('Inspect complete furniture construction and care',
'''Compare the frame, seams, cushion construction, cover removal, surface finish and accessible underside of each candidate. Obtain care instructions for the exact model and material. A removable cover may not be machine washable. A marketed performance fabric may still restrict the cleaning products the team normally uses.

Ask about replacement covers, inserts and parts, recording exact identifiers and current availability. Compare the landed replacement cost and the practical remedy if a component cannot be cleaned in time. Avoid presenting any fabric as scratch-proof, stain-proof or suitable for every pet. Product evidence and the local reset trial should drive the decision.'''),
('Connect textiles to the laundry process',
'''List each removable textile and the equipment or service that can clean and dry it under its instructions. Include furniture movement, removal, collection, drying and reinstallation. If a large washable rug requires off-site handling, the plan needs a responsible person, storage arrangement and an acceptable substitute or spare.

Keep pet-related cleaning procedures aligned with approved product methods. Refer uncertain sanitation, damage or health questions to appropriate professionals. The design brief should not invent chemical mixtures or substitute a decorating tip for a safe cleaning process. Record the cleaner's observations from a real reset and revise the specification where the workflow fails.'''),
('Plan guest supplies and owner stock',
'''Assign a consistent place for permitted guest supplies and keep spare inventory separate. Label towels or covers where the operating process calls for it, using a system that does not depend on one team member's memory. Ensure storage conditions suit the products and that supplies do not obstruct circulation or maintenance access.

Record quantities from actual use rather than adopting an unsupported universal stock formula. Track withdrawals, replacements and obsolete items. A spare cover from a discontinued sofa should not remain in active inventory as if it fits the replacement model. The inventory must describe the installed property.'''),
('Review condition without assuming causation',
'''Capture the accepted baseline condition and keep subsequent observations tied to the item and date. Distinguish routine wear, a particular damage incident and an unresolved source of odor or moisture. Follow the relevant operating and claim procedures rather than attributing every defect to the latest pet stay.

Use repeated observations to improve the design and care brief. If a surface consistently needs difficult repair, compare a different construction or a more serviceable component. Keep receipts, remedies and changed inventory together. Tax treatment of replacements or repairs remains a separate review using the actual records.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a host selects a sofa with removable seat covers, then discovers that the back upholstery requires a different cleaning process. The local team can wash the seats but cannot complete the back treatment between adjacent stays. The host reviews another model or arranges specialist support before purchasing. A matching spare is approved only after storage and compatibility are confirmed.''',
['Map pet-policy requirements to actual rooms and activities.','Save exact care and replacement-part documents.','Test the full textile removal and reset process.','Identify storage for guest supplies and compatible spares.','Keep condition, repair and replacement records item-specific.'],
['Item and permitted use','Model and material','Approved care method','Laundry or specialist owner','Compatible spare','Condition and replacement record'],
['/resources/materials/','/resources/maintenance/','/design-roadmap/tax-and-cost-segregation-records/'],['warranty','recalls'],True)
lesson('kitchen-inventory-group-meal-test','Test a rental kitchen with a complete group meal',
'Build kitchen inventory from preparation, serving, eating, cleanup and storage tasks, then verify the whole setup at the intended group size.',[
('Choose a meal scenario that matches the brief',
'''A collection of cookware is not a tested kitchen. Begin with a meal the property is intended to support and list preparation, cooking, serving, eating and cleanup tasks. The scenario should fit the stay and equipment offered. Avoid advertising a chef's kitchen merely because the room contains expensive appliances.

Identify which tasks occur together and who needs access to which surface. Record the installed appliances, their exact instructions and any limitations that should reach guests. Keep the intended service capacity separate from legal occupancy. Inventory quantities are planning decisions, not evidence that every group will cook or eat in the same way.'''),
('Build the inventory by task and storage location',
'''List the tools required for the scenario and place each in a realistic storage location. Include preparation equipment, compatible cookware, serving pieces, tableware and everyday cleaning supplies. Distinguish useful duplicates from items added simply to make a checklist look complete. Each addition needs space, care and a replacement route.

Check cookware compatibility with the installed equipment and follow manufacturer instructions. Save model information for appliances and component identifiers for frequently replaced items. A replacement lid or rack must match the actual product. Keep guest-accessible supplies separate from owner stock, chemicals and items that need controlled handling.'''),
('Simulate cooking and serving with the room occupied',
'''Use the actual table, chairs and appliance doors in their normal positions. Open the dishwasher and refrigerator while another person prepares food. Move serving pieces to the table and return used items for cleanup. Record congestion, inaccessible storage and the surfaces that become occupied too early.

The test may show a layout issue instead of an inventory shortage. Adding another countertop appliance can make the workflow worse when preparation space is already limited. Compare a change in storage or furniture placement before authorizing another purchase. Refer electrical, plumbing, ventilation or installation questions to the qualified person responsible.'''),
('Test cleanup and the next meal',
'''Run through where used dishes wait, how permitted items are washed and where they dry or return. Check dishwasher loading against the appliance instructions and the actual shapes of plates, glasses and cookware. Something described as dishwasher safe still needs to fit and be compatible with the selected washing process.

Review the scenario with the turnover team. Record which items need inspection, counting or special care. A practical reset should return the same usable kitchen, with important missing pieces visible in a short exception log. Avoid a stock system that requires counting dozens of decorative pieces while essential cookware remains unverified.'''),
('Keep the description and inventory aligned',
'''Document the accepted setup with cabinet photographs and a current inventory. Identify what guests can expect and explain unusual controls or restrictions clearly. After a replacement, update the operating guide and any listing details that materially change. The best source for an answer is the installed inventory rather than an old shopping list.

Track repeated guest questions and missing-item reports. Distinguish an absent essential item from a preference for a specialist tool outside the brief. Use that distinction to prioritize additions and keep storage coherent. Review actual failure and replacement records before standardizing a different product across a portfolio.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a rental advertised for eight guests has eight plates but only one small serving bowl, while a countertop appliance consumes the main preparation area. A dinner simulation exposes the bottleneck. The host revises the serving inventory and appliance placement, tests cleanup and photographs the accepted cabinet arrangement for the local team.''',
['Select a realistic meal and intended group size.','Inventory preparation, cooking, serving and cleanup separately.','Test appliance doors and table use at the same time.','Review washing compatibility and storage with the cleaner.','Reconcile the published kitchen description with the installed inventory.'],
['Meal task','Required item or equipment','Installed model and quantity','Storage location','Observed conflict','Accepted change'],
['/design-roadmap/kitchen-dining-and-gathering/','/resources/layouts/'],['amenities','warranty'])
lesson('rental-bedroom-nighttime-commissioning','Commission a bedroom after dark',
'Test a finished rental bedroom in its real sleeping configuration, including light, controls, luggage, bedding fit and bathroom routes.',[
('Prepare the room as guests will receive it',
'''A daytime installation photograph cannot show every sleep-related problem. Set up the actual bed, protector, linens, pillows, lamps, window treatments and storage before testing. Put ordinary luggage in the intended place. Record the final product models and any temporary substitutes so the result is tied to what was installed.

Inspect furniture and products according to their instructions. Do not improvise structural or electrical tests. If something appears damaged or unstable, have the responsible professional assess it before guest use. The nighttime review is an operating exercise, not a safety certification or a promise of comfort for every person.'''),
('Check the sequence from entry to bed',
'''Enter with the usual room lights in their normal state. Find the principal control, place a bag and locate charging. Check whether the route to the bed depends on moving a chair or stepping over cords. Repeat with the furniture used as intended, including open drawers or a luggage rack where applicable.

Lie or sit in the normal positions and reach the bedside controls. Test whether the light can be switched off without crossing a dark room. Record reflections, bright indicators and confusing controls. A useful lighting change follows an observed task; it should not be based only on how a fixture appears in a product photo.'''),
('Evaluate window coverage in the installed setting',
'''Observe the room at night and, where practical, in the morning conditions relevant to the stay. Check how the treatment operates, what happens at its edges and whether it interferes with doors, furniture or heating equipment. Follow the exact product and installation guidance, including any requirements relevant to cords or mounting.

Describe the result precisely. A room that feels dark during one test is not proof of total blackout in every season or weather condition. If the listing promises a particular feature, make sure the installed product and property evidence support the wording. Save the product details and a note about test conditions for future replacements.'''),
('Test bedding and reset together',
'''Check the bedding with the actual mattress and base, including how the fitted sheet, protector and cover fit. Record the sizes and care instructions rather than assuming that a retail size name ensures compatibility. Follow the manufacturer's requirements for the frame and mattress pairing.

Have the turnover team make and reset the bed. Check whether furniture obstructs access and whether spare linens can be found without moving guest belongings. Label storage by room or compatible size. If the care process requires off-site handling or drying beyond the usual schedule, make that dependency explicit before approving the inventory.'''),
('Walk the bathroom route and close the record',
'''Use the ordinary night lighting and walk from the bed to the relevant bathroom. Note changes in level, door interactions and controls. Provide accurate property information rather than claiming a route suits every guest. Building and accessibility conclusions need the appropriate qualified review.

Keep a commissioning log with the condition, observation, owner, action and retest. Close an issue after the actual remedy is checked, then update the room guide and reset photograph. Revisit the log after early stays to identify repeated functional questions. A successful test supports a specific arrangement; it does not establish that design caused a booking or review outcome.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a finished bedroom looks complete during the day. After dark, the only switch reachable from bed operates a decorative light, and luggage blocks the route to the bathroom. The owner revises the layout and obtains an appropriate lighting solution, then repeats the nighttime walk with the actual luggage and linens present.''',
['Use installed products and record temporary substitutes.','Enter, unpack, charge and turn off lights as a guest would.','Observe window coverage and note test conditions.','Complete a bed-making and spare-linen reset.','Retest the bathroom route after changes.'],
['Test condition and time','Guest task','Observation','Product or room reference','Action owner','Retest evidence'],
['/resources/comfort/','/resources/lighting/'],['lighting','amenities','recalls'])
lesson('rental-accessibility-feature-evidence','Document accessibility features with useful property evidence',
'Create an accurate feature record with photographs, measurements, routes and unresolved questions, while keeping listing evidence separate from compliance conclusions.',[
('Start with specific features and questions',
'''Accessibility needs differ. A general label cannot tell a guest whether a particular entrance, route, bathroom or bedroom meets their needs. Start with the actual feature being described and the evidence required to explain it. Record what exists, not what the owner hopes a guest can manage.

Airbnb provides guidance for presenting accessibility features and supporting photographs. Review the current platform requirements when preparing a submission. Keep those listing requirements distinct from building-code or legal obligations, which depend on the property and applicable rules. A platform feature review is not a universal compliance certificate.'''),
('Trace a complete guest journey',
'''Connect parking or arrival, entrance, relevant rooms, bathroom and outdoor features on a simple route map. Record changes in level, door configurations and material conditions that affect the path. Photograph the relationships between areas rather than showing only an isolated feature that cannot be reached by the same route.

Use a consistent measurement method and units. Identify the person, date and configuration measured. A doorway measurement with the door in one position may not explain the usable opening in another. Mark uncertain or unmeasured facts explicitly. Obtain qualified advice for technical interpretation or proposed alterations instead of turning a rough survey into a compliance claim.'''),
('Photograph the feature in context',
'''Take clear images that show what the guest needs to evaluate: the approach, relevant opening, surface, installed equipment and relationship to adjacent space. Include useful context without concealing an obstruction through staging. Save the image date and connect it to the property record.

A movable piece of furniture can alter the route after the photograph. Specify the standard reset arrangement and communicate it to the local team. If a feature is temporarily unavailable, update the listing information through the operating process. An older approved photograph should not remain the only evidence for a changed property.'''),
('Separate verified facts from professional conclusions',
'''Use the Access Board's technical guides as background where applicable, while asking a qualified professional to determine the requirements for this property. Do not assume one dimension, fixture or product makes an entire rental accessible. Avoid installing improvised supports or describing decorative hardware as assistive equipment.

For each listing statement, record the evidence and whether professional interpretation is needed. A guest question may ask for a measurement or a photograph rather than a broad assurance. Answer using the current record and say clearly when a fact remains unverified. This is more useful than guessing about an individual guest's abilities.'''),
('Maintain feature information after changes',
'''Add feature evidence to the installation handover and room reset instructions. Flag purchases that might affect a route or controls. When furniture is replaced, repeat the relevant survey and compare the result with the listing description. Keep the earlier version so the change can be traced.

Give the person maintaining the listing responsibility for updating images and text. Review recurring questions as signals that a feature needs clearer information. The record should help guests make informed decisions and help the owner manage accuracy. It does not replace a professional assessment of the property or its obligations.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a listing photograph shows an unobstructed entrance, but a new console narrows the route from that entrance to the bedroom. The owner measures the actual arrangement, updates the reset plan and retakes the relevant photographs. The description reports the verified features instead of retaining a broad claim based on the previous layout.''',
['Identify the exact feature or guest question.','Trace arrival-to-room relationships.','Record measurement method, date and configuration.','Keep listing evidence separate from compliance assessment.','Recheck affected features after furnishing changes.'],
['Feature or route','Verified observation','Measurement and method','Photograph reference','Professional question','Listing update owner'],
['/design-roadmap/measured-layout-and-delivery-access/','/resources/layouts/'],['accessibility','routes'])
lesson('turnover-time-design-study','Use a turnover observation to improve the design',
'Observe the real reset process to find furnishing, storage and care problems, then test a specific design remedy without inventing a labor-saving forecast.',[
('Define what the observation should answer',
'''A turnover study is most useful when it addresses a particular question: why linen replacement is difficult, why furniture must be moved, where supplies are hard to find or which surface needs repeated attention. Agree on the purpose with the local team and observe ordinary work with their knowledge. Avoid framing the exercise as a judgment about an individual cleaner's speed.

Record the property condition, tasks included, team size and any unusual events. A heavily used stay and a lightly used stay are not automatically comparable. Keep the observation descriptive and distinguish actual measured durations from estimates. The aim is to understand how the design interacts with work, not to create a universal productivity benchmark.'''),
('Follow the movement of people and materials',
'''Trace clean linens, used linens, supplies, waste and replacement items through the property. Note stairs, locked doors, long carries and repeated returns to storage. Record where a task pauses because an item is missing or another activity occupies the space. These dependencies often reveal more than the total elapsed time.

Observe furniture access and product care. A rug may be easy to vacuum until a heavy coffee table blocks removal; a bed may be straightforward to make until it is tightly placed between walls. Use exact product instructions when evaluating care. Refer damage, moisture, electrical or other technical concerns to the appropriate professional.'''),
('Classify the friction before choosing a remedy',
'''Separate layout, inventory, instructions, equipment, product care and scheduling issues. A missing spare sheet needs a different response from a mattress that is difficult to reach. A slow-drying textile may need a different care plan or specification rather than another label on the shelf.

For each issue, write the observed condition, its consequence and a candidate remedy. Include the complete cost and any downstream effect. Moving stock closer to a bedroom may help access but consume guest storage or create a security concern. Evaluate the property as a whole instead of optimizing one task at the expense of another.'''),
('Run a limited, comparable trial',
'''Change one defined element where practical, then repeat the relevant task under documented conditions. Keep team size, task scope and ordinary supplies visible in the record. If several things change at once, describe the result as a combined trial rather than assigning all improvement to one purchase.

Collect the team's feedback about reach, clarity, effort and consistency alongside measured time. A slightly faster arrangement may be less reliable or harder to maintain. Use the trial to decide whether the remedy is worth adopting. Do not extrapolate one observation into guaranteed annual savings, fewer complaints or higher revenue.'''),
('Update the operating standard and financial record',
'''When a remedy is accepted, revise the inventory, care instructions and room reset photographs. Record which old instruction is superseded and who receives the new version. Keep open issues assigned. A successful observation is only useful if the normal operating team can repeat the improved process.

Preserve receipts and a description of any furniture, storage or other work purchased for the remedy. Keep repair and replacement facts traceable for the accountant's later review; the observation does not determine tax treatment. Review the adopted change after additional use to catch unintended effects that the first trial did not reveal.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a cleaner returns downstairs three times to obtain differently sized bedding. The host records the storage route and tests labeled room-level linen storage during another comparable reset. The trial includes team feedback and actual task scope. The owner adopts the arrangement only after confirming that guest storage and secure owner stock still work.''',
['Agree on the observation purpose and conditions.','Trace supplies, linens, waste and repeated journeys.','Classify the issue before purchasing a remedy.','Record comparable task scope during the trial.','Update reset instructions and preserve purchase records.'],
['Task and conditions','Observed movement or delay','Issue category','Candidate remedy and cost','Trial result','Accepted operating change'],
['/resources/maintenance/','/design-roadmap/tax-and-cost-segregation-records/'],['warranty','amenities'],True)
lesson('bathroom-refresh-design-brief','Prepare a bathroom refresh brief before selecting finishes',
'Separate cosmetic changes, product replacement and specialist work, then coordinate storage, cleaning access, installation and an accurate project record.',[
('Record the problem the refresh must solve',
'''Start with the actual bathroom condition and repeated operating issues. A dated finish, inadequate storage, confusing lighting, a recurring moisture concern and a plumbing defect are different problems. List each separately with evidence and a person responsible for further review. Do not let a finish board obscure a building condition that needs investigation.

Record what remains: fixtures, cabinetry, flooring, wall finishes, lighting and accessories. Photograph the condition and keep product information where identifiable. Establish whether the project is a cosmetic refresh or involves work requiring professional assessment, permits or specialist installation. Obtain the property-specific requirements from the appropriate local professionals before promising a completion scope.'''),
('Test the bathroom as a sequence of activities',
'''Walk through entering, using the sink, setting down toiletries, bathing, drying, dressing and cleaning. Open the vanity, shower and room doors in normal use. Record where towels, personal items and spare supplies will go. A clear storage plan can be more valuable than another decorative accessory.

Check the lighting and controls in the actual mirror position and under ordinary evening conditions. Identify whether the proposed change is a furnishing choice or electrical work. The same distinction applies to mounted accessories and support equipment. A product's appearance does not establish that its installation or intended use suits the property.'''),
('Evaluate materials against the care process',
'''Collect exact product specifications and care instructions for candidate finishes. Compare seams, edges, grout or joints, access for cleaning and replacement availability. A low-maintenance label is not a complete cleaning procedure. Ask the local team how the selected product would be cleaned and what equipment or specialist service it requires.

Persistent moisture deserves attention beyond decorating. EPA's guidance emphasizes identifying and correcting the moisture problem; covering a symptom does not resolve its cause. Refer suspected defects and remediation questions to qualified providers. The design brief should record the investigation and approved scope rather than prescribing a generic repair based on a photograph.'''),
('Coordinate the work and return-to-use decision',
'''Map dependencies between demolition, repair, preparation, installation, finishing and final checks. Confirm who supplies the products and who verifies compatibility. Get the relevant manufacturer and contractor instructions for curing, use and cleaning, rather than assuming a universal waiting period.

Plan how the property operates while the bathroom is unavailable. The manager needs to know which rooms and advertised features are affected. A bathroom count in the listing must reflect actual availability through the operating process. Keep completion, cleaning, functional checks and guest readiness distinct; an invoice alone is not evidence that the room is ready.'''),
('Keep the installed and financial handoff connected',
'''Request useful descriptions of the work and products in vendor invoices. Link each line to the property and room, retaining payments, credits and approved changes. Keep removed and installed items distinguishable in the project record. Do not invent cost allocations or classify work for tax purposes based only on a design term such as refresh.

At handover, save final photographs, model identifiers, care instructions and any unresolved punch-list items. Give the local team the accepted reset arrangement. The tax preparer and, where relevant, the cost segregation provider can use the organized records to identify further questions; the design brief itself does not determine treatment.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: an owner plans to replace a vanity because its lower edge is swollen. The condition record identifies a possible moisture issue, which is assessed before new cabinetry is ordered. The final scope separates the professional repair, the installed vanity and the accessories. Invoices and photographs remain linked to the bathroom record for operating and financial handoff.''',
['Separate appearance, storage, care and technical defects.','Walk all ordinary bathroom activities and door positions.','Review exact finish instructions with the cleaning team.','Assign technical assessment and installation responsibilities.','Preserve itemized work records and readiness evidence.'],
['Existing condition and evidence','Desired function','Retained or replaced item','Specialist review and scope','Product and care instructions','Invoice and completion reference'],
['/design-roadmap/concept-color-and-materials/','/design-roadmap/tax-and-cost-segregation-records/'],['moisture','warranty'],True)
lesson('wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan','Design an entry for wet gear and everyday arrivals',
'Plan bags, footwear, coats and outdoor equipment around the actual arrival route, drying needs, cleaning access and seasonal operating process.',[
('Observe what arrives at this property',
'''An entry needs to serve the stay rather than an abstract style. List the bags, footwear, coats and equipment that guests are likely to bring under the property's actual brief. A lake house, ski-area rental and city apartment can have different storage requirements, but each still needs a clear arrival route.

Walk from unloading to the entrance and through the first occupied space. Record doors, steps, lighting, floor changes and where somebody can place an item while opening the next door. Do not assume a decorative bench or narrow console solves arrival. Test with the relevant luggage or equipment and keep any technical access requirements with qualified review.'''),
('Separate arrival, storage and drying',
'''These are related but different functions. Guests may need a temporary place to set down a bag, a secure or practical place to store equipment and an appropriate process for wet items. Assign a location to each. Avoid allowing drying stock to consume the only route through the entry.

Choose products and surfaces using exact manufacturer instructions and the expected exposure. A material described as durable is not necessarily appropriate for repeated wet storage. Record floor and furniture care requirements, then review them with the local team. Treat recurring dampness or building moisture as a separate professional assessment, rather than adding a decorative mat to conceal it.'''),
('Make the storage usable with people present',
'''Test benches, doors, drawers and hooks while another person enters or sits. Record whether bags interfere with a door swing or whether a storage lid requires moving everyone nearby. Compare the installed depth and projection of wall items, not just their capacity in a product listing.

Use suitable installation for the actual product, wall and expected use. Follow manufacturer instructions and obtain professional mounting where required. Do not represent an improvised hook or shelf as suitable for heavy equipment. Keep allowed uses clear and avoid creating a guest task that depends on unclear or unsafe handling.'''),
('Connect the plan to cleaning and seasonal care',
'''Identify how the entry is reset after wet or dirty arrivals. Record where permitted cleaning supplies live, who handles drying items and which materials need particular care. Include storage for owner equipment and guest supplies without mixing them into one unidentified pile.

Develop the seasonal changeover with the local manager. A summer equipment shelf might not fit winter stock, and an outdoor drying arrangement may be unavailable during certain weather. State the ordinary setup and the exception process. The plan should remain usable when the owner is away and should not depend on guests guessing where wet items belong.'''),
('Use feedback to refine the arrangement',
'''Review repeated questions, misplaced equipment and observed congestion. Classify each issue as a missing location, unclear instruction, insufficient capacity or unsuitable product. Choose the smallest appropriate remedy and test it in the full arrival sequence.

Update room photographs, the inventory and guest guidance after an accepted change. Preserve receipts and product information for replacements. Measure success through observed usability and a repeatable reset; do not infer an increase in booking value from an attractive entry photograph alone.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a four-bedroom retreat provides a bench with internal storage, but guests cannot open it while the entry is occupied. Wet bags collect beside the main door. The owner tests a different allocation of footwear and equipment storage, reviews surface care and confirms that the arrival route remains clear during an ordinary group arrival.''',
['List actual arrival items and expected exposure.','Assign separate arrival, storage and drying locations.','Test doors and storage with people and bags present.','Review product suitability and mounting instructions.','Document ordinary and seasonal reset arrangements.'],
['Arrival item','Temporary placement','Storage or drying location','Exposure and care requirement','Installation reference','Reset owner'],
['/resources/outdoors/','/resources/layouts/'],['moisture','warranty'])
lesson('portfolio-furnishing-standard-with-local-variation','Build a portfolio furnishing standard that allows useful variation',
'Standardize records, serviceable components and approvals across rentals while allowing each property’s layout, guests and exposure to determine the final specification.',[
('Standardize the decision process first',
'''A portfolio standard does not need to make every rental look identical. Start with common records: room identifiers, product schedules, care documents, purchasing approvals and installation evidence. These make projects easier to compare while preserving differences in layout, guest brief and existing finishes.

Define which decisions are centrally controlled and which remain property-specific. Mattress compatibility, delivery access and outdoor exposure cannot be resolved from a portfolio brand board alone. Record the evidence needed for a local exception. The aim is a repeatable method, not automatic purchasing of the same object regardless of where it will be used.'''),
('Create a product standard with compatible parts',
'''For each approved product or family, record the model, revision, finish, dimensions, instructions, replacement components and current supplier. Distinguish an exact match from an alternative that merely looks similar. Left and right sectional parts, different bed-frame revisions and textile sizes can create expensive mistakes when pooled casually.

Ask about warranty terms and use conditions for the actual rental application. Keep the written terms with the product record. A portfolio order or a commercial label does not establish coverage. Recheck availability and instructions when a model changes; do not allow the old record to become a false promise of future compatibility.'''),
("Allow exceptions for the property’s real constraints",
'''Use a short exception form that states the standard item, the local constraint and the proposed replacement. Compare assembled and packed dimensions, care, access, lead time and complete landed cost. Name who approves the exception and which records must change.

An exception should preserve the function and operating requirements wherever possible. If a standard sofa cannot reach a room, the replacement still needs suitable seating, care and serviceability. A local design preference can be considered separately from a necessary fit or exposure change. Keeping those reasons distinct helps future teams understand the decision.'''),
('Control shared stock and transfers',
'''Label spare inventory by exact compatibility and location. Keep each property's owned or assigned inventory identifiable. When items move between properties, record the transfer, condition, source and destination. A central closet full of unlabeled cushions and hardware is not a reliable spare-parts system.

Review actual usage, storage conditions and lead times before purchasing more stock. Remove obsolete or incompatible items from active availability records. A part listed as available must be findable and suitable for the installed model. Keep stock control connected to replacements so the accounting and operating records describe the same events.'''),
('Learn from failures without losing property context',
'''Collect consistent issue records: item, age or installation date, observed defect, remedy, cost and interruption. Distinguish supplier changes, installation errors and property-specific exposure. A problem at one rental may justify review without proving that every copy should be replaced immediately.

Preserve itemized purchases, transfers, credits and disposal or replacement records for the accountant's review. Keep property-level project evidence separate if a study provider needs it. Standardized data can support that handoff, but it does not decide tax classifications or eliminate the need to reconcile each property's records.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a portfolio uses the same sofa family in four rentals. One property has a narrow stair landing, so its approved exception uses a different construction. The inventory lists that sofa separately, and its covers are not pooled with the other properties. A later replacement order follows the installed model rather than the original portfolio board.''',
['Define common records and property-specific decisions.','Track exact product revisions and compatible parts.','Approve exceptions using fit, care and landed cost.','Record stock locations and inter-property transfers.','Review failure data with property context and separate financial records.'],
['Portfolio standard item','Installed property and model','Local constraint or exception','Compatible spare and location','Transfer or replacement event','Approval and financial reference'],
['/design-roadmap/procurement-receiving-and-claims/','/design-roadmap/tax-and-cost-segregation-records/'],['warranty','recalls'],True)
lesson('furnishing-project-critical-path','Build a furnishing schedule from dependencies',
'Separate decisions, deliveries, site readiness and installation so the launch schedule reflects the actual sequence and unresolved assumptions.',[
('Define completion in terms of guest functions',
'''A project schedule needs an explicit end condition. List the rooms, functions and evidence required before the owner or manager can decide guest readiness. Furniture arrival, installation completion, photography and listing publication are different milestones. Treating them as one date hides dependencies and makes a delayed item harder to evaluate.

Identify essential functions and optional styling separately. A missing decorative object and an unusable bed have different implications. Name the person who makes the readiness decision and what they need to see. Use the actual operating requirements rather than an advertised installation duration borrowed from another property.'''),
('Work backward through the dependencies',
'''For each final check, list what must happen first. A room photograph follows cleaning and styling; styling follows the installed layout; installation may depend on flooring, paint preparation or trade work. Purchasing depends on approved specifications and verified critical measurements. Write these relationships before assigning dates.

The critical path is the chain of tasks whose delay moves the required completion milestone, given the current plan. It can change as information changes. Record it as a planning relationship, not a prediction that every task will occur on time. Ask the responsible vendors and trades for the actual constraints affecting their work.'''),
('Separate lead-time estimates from commitments',
'''Record the source and date of delivery information. A product listing's dispatch estimate, a vendor's current stock statement and a confirmed delivery appointment are different evidence. Keep them in separate fields so a placeholder does not become a firm installation assumption through repeated copying.

Include receiving, inspection, storage and remedy time where relevant. A shipment arriving at a warehouse is not the same as an accepted item in its final room. Review how a damaged or wrong item affects the dependency chain. Identify the person authorized to approve a substitute and the conditions under which it can be ordered.'''),
('Use decision deadlines to protect the sequence',
'''Assign dates for measurements, selections, approvals, order release, site readiness, receiving and acceptance checks. A decision deadline should indicate what becomes affected if it is missed. Keep the approver available with the evidence needed to decide, rather than relying on an informal message during an installation visit.

Build exception paths for important uncertainties. A temporary solution must still meet the property's intended use and be accurately communicated to the manager and photographer. Do not assume guests can work around an essential function that is unresolved. Review the launch decision when delays change the actual available setup.'''),
('Update costs and records with schedule changes',
'''Track extra storage, freight, visits, labor and other costs caused by an accepted change. Keep the reason and approval with the revised schedule. A delayed installation may also require new photography or a changed cleaning plan. Tell each affected person which schedule version applies.

At handover, preserve the actual chronology alongside invoices and installed inventory. Distinguish ordered, delivered, installed and ready-for-use events. That record is useful for operating review and financial questions, but a scheduling label does not establish a tax placed-in-service conclusion. The preparer determines treatment from the supported facts.''')],
'''Illustrative scenario: a sofa is scheduled to arrive after the intended photography day. Its receiving inspection and installation also require separate appointments. The owner maps those dependencies, obtains current vendor information and changes the photo plan or approves a suitable alternative after fit and cost review. The schedule no longer treats an estimated dispatch date as completed installation.''',
['Define essential completion evidence for each room.','Map predecessors before assigning dates.','Keep estimated dispatch, receiving and installation distinct.','Assign approval deadlines and exception owners.','Record actual chronology and cost effects.'],
['Milestone','Required predecessor','Evidence and date confidence','Decision owner','Delay or exception effect','Actual completion record'],
['/design-roadmap/installation-photography-and-launch/','/design-roadmap/tax-and-cost-segregation-records/'],['warranty'],True)
# Each lab and FAQ is authored for its lesson; it is not a property-name substitution.
LABS={
'large-group-activity-zoning':('Run a breakfast-to-evening zoning lab',
'''Print the floor plan and use a separate marker for cooking, eating, conversation, work and quiet time. First mark breakfast with the entire intended group present. Then mark an evening when some guests watch a screen and others read or go to bed. Write down every location that serves two incompatible functions at once. Include furniture in use rather than showing all chairs pushed neatly under tables.

For each conflict, propose two changes. One should use the existing inventory if feasible; the other may involve a purchase or a different room function. Compare the effects on seating, routes, storage, cleaning and the whole-property budget. A solution that adds a work surface but removes the only luggage space needs another review. Re-run both activity periods using the revised arrangement.

Finish with a short acceptance statement: which activities the property supports together, which configuration is standard and what limitation remains. Give that statement to the listing and operating owners. It is more useful than a broad claim that a large house is perfect for every group.'''),
'studio-rental-zoning-without-extra-furniture':('Run a two-configuration footprint lab',
'''Make two copies of the measured plan. On the first, show the normal daytime arrangement with bags, dining or work seating and the kitchenette in use. On the second, show the sleeping arrangement with the convertible mechanism open, bedding present and the route to the bathroom. Use the manufacturer's actual dimensions and verify important uncertainties on site.

Circle the items that must move between configurations. For each, record its destination, who moves it and whether the operating instructions explain the change. Test the movement physically where appropriate, following the product instructions. Include where cushions, leaves, bedding and other displaced parts are stored; a plan that makes them disappear is incomplete.

Compare an option that removes a nonessential piece with an option that buys a smaller replacement. Record the full cost and the function retained by each. Accept the solution only after both room configurations work. Keep the two plans with the room reset record so future replacements do not recreate the same conflict.'''),
'multigenerational-stay-design-brief':('Run a choice-and-information lab',
'''Write six neutral guest tasks: finding a quiet seat, reaching the relevant bathroom, setting down a bag, preparing breakfast, using a light and locating spare bedding. Match each task to the actual property features. Where guests have more than one option, describe the difference without assigning it to an age group or presumed ability.

For each task, identify which information a guest would need before booking and which belongs in the arrival guide. A stair relationship or bathroom distribution can affect the booking decision; a clearly labeled linen shelf may be an arrival detail. Support important facts with current photographs or measurements and label unresolved questions.

Ask an unfamiliar reviewer to use the record to answer three practical questions about the property. Record where the information was missing or ambiguous and revise it. The reviewer is checking clarity, not certifying accessibility or representing every family. Preserve the specific feature descriptions and the route to qualified review for technical questions.'''),
'pet-friendly-furnishing-system':('Run a cover-to-reset compatibility lab',
'''Select one upholstered item and collect the exact instructions for the furniture, cover, insert and any approved cleaning product. Put the model and revision on a single care card. Mark which components are removable, which can be cleaned by the local team and which require another service. Treat missing instructions as an open approval issue.

Walk the complete reset sequence using a spare sample or permitted product trial where appropriate. Include removal, transport, permitted cleaning, drying, reassembly and storage. Record the equipment and responsible person at each step. Do not conduct an experimental chemical treatment or assume that one component's instructions apply to the whole item.

Now compare two interruption scenarios: a compatible spare is available, or the component must be ordered. List the actual operational choices and costs to confirm. The lab should end with a care and exception process, not a claim that the product cannot be damaged. Keep its receipts and replacement identifiers with the installed inventory.'''),
'kitchen-inventory-group-meal-test':('Run a preparation-to-cleanup inventory lab',
'''Write the selected meal across a sheet in five columns: preparation, cooking, serving, eating and cleanup. Under each, list required items and their actual storage locations. Mark an item used in more than one stage so a shared bowl or surface does not appear available twice at the same time. Follow equipment instructions throughout the test.

Set out the required tableware and cookware in the real room. Note where the group would sit and where used items return. Record a missing tool, an access conflict and an unnecessary item as different findings. The appropriate response may be a purchase, a storage change or removal of something that consumes useful space.

Prepare a final cabinet map using the accepted inventory. Give the cleaner a short check of the important items and an exception process. Retain exact product information for anything that needs compatible replacements. Repeat the lab when a major appliance, table or storage arrangement changes rather than assuming the old capacity still applies.'''),
'rental-bedroom-nighttime-commissioning':('Run a bedside-to-bathroom test lab',
'''Prepare a room record with the mattress, frame, linens, lights, window treatments and storage actually installed. Ask the reviewer to enter with ordinary luggage, find the main light, place personal items, reach charging and settle into the sleeping configuration. Record test conditions and the sequence of observations.

Then follow the nighttime bathroom route using the normal controls. Identify a confusing action, a physical obstruction and a product defect separately. The first may need clearer instructions, the second a layout review and the third a repair or replacement. Do not treat instructions as an acceptable remedy for an unresolved technical problem.

Write a targeted retest for each accepted action. If a lamp changes, check the relevant controls and placement; if furniture moves, repeat the route and luggage test. Update the reset photograph after the final arrangement is accepted. A closed record should describe what was tested, by whom and under which conditions, while acknowledging that individual comfort preferences vary.'''),
'rental-accessibility-feature-evidence':('Run a statement-to-evidence review lab',
'''Copy each proposed feature statement into a worksheet without changing its wording. Beside it, list the current photograph, measurement or product document that supports it. Add the date, the measured configuration and the person who prepared the evidence. Leave a blank evidence field visibly unresolved rather than supplying a plausible assumption.

Trace the route needed to use the feature. A documented bathroom fixture does not explain how someone reaches that bathroom. Record relevant room relationships and any change in level, and identify where qualified interpretation is required. Keep platform listing requirements and property-specific professional review in separate columns.

Ask the listing owner to verify that the published wording matches the supported fact. Revise broad assurances into specific descriptions where appropriate, using the current platform process. Schedule a new review when furniture, surfaces or equipment change. The completed lab is an accuracy record; it is not a finding that a particular guest can use the property or that all obligations are satisfied.'''),
'turnover-time-design-study':('Run an observation-to-remedy lab',
'''Choose one reset task and define exactly where it starts and ends. Record the team, supplies, property condition and any unusual event. Observe the route and interruptions, then separate active work from waiting or searching. Keep estimated time clearly labeled if no measurement was taken.

Propose one remedy and list the functions it might affect elsewhere. Moving linens closer to a room, for example, changes storage allocation and access. Compare a layout or instruction change before assuming a product purchase is necessary. Record the proposed cost and the evidence needed to decide whether the change worked.

Repeat the same defined task under documented conditions where feasible. Compare the observed outcome and team feedback without claiming a controlled experiment. If the trial is accepted, update the reset standard and financial records. Plan another review after ordinary use so the first result does not become a permanent assumption that nobody checks.'''),
'bathroom-refresh-design-brief':('Run a cosmetic-versus-technical scope lab',
'''Make a room photograph set and assign an identifier to each observed problem. Classify it as appearance, storage, care, product function or technical investigation. Keep uncertainty explicit: an owner may observe swelling or staining without knowing its cause. Assign investigation rather than selecting a finish to cover the symptom.

For each approved change, record the exact product, required preparation, installer, dependencies and completion evidence. Compare the proposed scope with the current room inventory. Note what is removed, what remains and what must be updated in the care and listing records. Obtain property-specific requirements and instructions from the responsible professionals.

Finally reconcile the proposal to the financial record you expect to receive. Request useful work descriptions and keep bundled or unclear amounts as questions for the vendor and accountant. The scope lab should make the operating and financial handoff clearer while leaving tax conclusions with the preparer and relevant property analysis with the study provider.'''),
'wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan':('Run an occupied-arrival lab',
'''Use the ordinary arrival route with the bags and equipment described in the brief. Test entry while one person is opening storage and another is setting down belongings. Record doors and containers in use, not only in their closed position. Photograph the conflict before deciding whether another piece of furniture is needed.

Assign temporary placement, longer-term storage and drying separately. Compare those locations with cleaning access and the normal guest route. Check product suitability and follow installation instructions for any proposed hook, shelf or rack. Flag building moisture and technical concerns for appropriate review rather than making them part of a decorating experiment.

Repeat the arrival with the revised arrangement, then ask the local team to reset it. Include the seasonal exception when an outdoor location cannot be used. Write the accepted locations into the guest and operating guides. A useful final arrangement is understandable during a busy arrival and can be restored without the owner explaining it in person.'''),
'portfolio-furnishing-standard-with-local-variation':('Run a standard-to-exception lab',
'''Choose a portfolio item and gather its current specification, parts information and actual installed property list. Compare the model identifiers rather than matching photographs. Mark any revision or finish difference that could affect dimensions, care or spare compatibility.

Test the standard against one property-specific constraint, such as access, exposure or retained furniture. Prepare two options: the exact standard if it fits, and a documented exception. Compare the function, installed cost, care, replacement support and downstream inventory changes. The exception should have a stated reason and approver, not simply a different shopping link.

Update the spare and financial records for the accepted choice. Record transfers between properties and remove obsolete compatibility assumptions. At the next review, compare actual failure records while retaining local context. This lab supports consistent management without implying that one product must suit every building or that pooled purchases receive a single tax treatment.'''),
'furnishing-project-critical-path':('Run a delivery-delay schedule lab',
'''Select one important product and place its current milestones on the schedule: specification approval, order, estimated dispatch, receiving, inspection, installation and final check. Label each date with its evidence and confidence. Keep the current vendor information attached to the line.

Move receiving later in a hypothetical delay scenario and trace every affected predecessor and successor. Record which tasks can still proceed, which visits must move and what extra storage or labor needs a quote. A task is not independent merely because a different person performs it. Review the whole chain to the readiness decision.

Prepare an exception path with an approver and required fit, care and cost checks. If a substitute is accepted, update the purchasing register, layout, inventory and photography brief. Retain the actual chronology at closeout. The exercise helps the team make decisions under uncertainty; it does not promise a universal furnishing timeline or determine tax timing.'''),
}
FAQS={
'large-group-activity-zoning':[
('Should every room seat the full guest count?','No single rule fits every brief. The main gathering and dining arrangements should match what the property represents, while secondary spaces can serve different activities. Test the whole stay, describe meaningful limitations and avoid counting isolated chairs as equivalent seats in one usable group configuration.'),
('Can outdoor seating solve an indoor capacity problem?','It can provide another option, but its usefulness depends on weather, maintenance and the property brief. Record how meals and gathering work when the outdoor area is unavailable. Do not make an unstated assumption that guests will always split into separate spaces.'),
('How do I know whether a zoning change worked?','Repeat the activity that exposed the conflict and record whether the accepted arrangement resolves it. Use local-team feedback and repeated guest questions as additional observations. Booking changes require a separate analysis because pricing, seasonality, availability and marketing can also change.')],
'studio-rental-zoning-without-extra-furniture':[
('Is a sofa bed always the best studio choice?','It depends on the desired stay, product instructions, available room and reset process. Compare both open and closed configurations with storage and routes included. A fixed bed or a different layout may fit the brief better; the decision should follow the actual test.'),
('Can one surface serve dining and work?','It can if the stay supports sharing it at different times. Check what happens when activities overlap and where equipment or tableware goes during the change. Describe the installed feature accurately so a guest does not infer a separate dedicated workspace that is not present.'),
('Should I add mirrors to make the room look larger?','Evaluate mirrors as actual products with placement, mounting, cleaning and reflection requirements. They do not create physical circulation or storage capacity. Resolve the functional plan first, then test the visual effect in the room rather than relying on a universal styling rule.')],
'multigenerational-stay-design-brief':[
('Does multigenerational mean accessible?','No. The terms describe different things. State verified features, stairs, routes and room relationships specifically, and obtain appropriate professional review for technical obligations. Guests should be able to evaluate their own needs using accurate information rather than a broad assurance.'),
('Should I create separate spaces for different ages?','Create activity choices instead of assigning behavior by age. A quiet seat, a work surface and a group gathering area may help many guests. Let the room arrangement, actual stay brief and usable circulation determine what can be provided.'),
('Which details belong in the listing?','Information that materially affects suitability before booking should be clear there, supported by current feature evidence. Ordinary operation details can also appear in the arrival guide. Keep both current when furniture or equipment changes and answer specific questions with verified facts.')],
'pet-friendly-furnishing-system':[
('Does removable upholstery mean washable upholstery?','No. Removal and cleaning permissions are separate product facts. Read the instructions for the exact cover, insert and surrounding furniture, and confirm that the local equipment and service can follow them. Mark missing or contradictory instructions as an open selection question.'),
('How many spare covers should I buy?','Use actual compatibility, available storage, observed replacement needs and supplier lead times. A spare is useful only if it can be found, fits the installed revision and can be maintained appropriately. Avoid treating a general quantity rule as evidence for your property.'),
('Should pet-related replacements be classified as repairs?','The design team should preserve what was purchased, what changed, the invoices and relevant dates. The accountant determines the applicable treatment from those facts. A pet-friendly label or an operating damage description does not by itself establish a tax classification.')],
'kitchen-inventory-group-meal-test':[
('Is one plate per guest enough?','The appropriate inventory depends on the meal plan, washing process, storage and intended experience. Test the selected scenario rather than treating a bare count as proof of a complete kitchen. Record how items move through preparation, serving, cleanup and the next use.'),
('Should I add every popular countertop appliance?','Only when it serves the brief and its space, care and operating requirements can be supported. Evaluate the preparation area, instructions, cleaning and replacement needs. An appliance that crowds the only useful work surface may reduce the kitchen’s practical function.'),
('How should the cleaner check the kitchen?','Use an accepted cabinet map and a concise list of important usable items, with a process for missing or damaged pieces. Include model-specific care and compatibility information where relevant. Keep the check attainable so decorative counting does not obscure essential functions.')],
'rental-bedroom-nighttime-commissioning':[
('Can I verify blackout during one visit?','Record the conditions and the observed result rather than making a universal claim. Product specifications and installation matter, and daylight can vary. Use supported wording in the listing and recheck relevant conditions when treatments or room arrangements change.'),
('Should the commissioning test replace an inspection?','No. It is an ordinary-use review of the installed arrangement. It does not certify electrical, structural, building or other technical requirements. Have the responsible qualified person resolve defects and specialist questions, then retest the affected guest task.'),
('What happens after the first stays?','Review repeated functional questions, care problems and observed layout issues. Connect them to the commissioning log and test a defined remedy. Keep individual comfort preferences distinct from a verified product defect or a recurring operating problem.')],
'rental-accessibility-feature-evidence':[
('Does a platform-approved feature certify the whole property?','No. A platform feature review and a professional determination about property obligations are different processes. Preserve specific evidence, use accurate wording and ask the appropriate professional to interpret technical requirements for the actual property.'),
('What if I cannot verify a dimension remotely?','Keep it marked as unverified and assign a local measurement or professional review. Do not infer it from a wide-angle photograph or another unit’s plan. Record the method, configuration and date when the measurement is obtained.'),
('How often should feature evidence be reviewed?','Review it when furniture, surfaces, equipment or routes change, and when a question reveals unclear information. Set an operating owner for updates. The relevant trigger is a possible change in accuracy, rather than assuming old images remain sufficient indefinitely.')],
'turnover-time-design-study':[
('Can one faster reset establish annual savings?','No. One observation has a specific team, property condition and task scope. Record the result and limitations, and gather more comparable evidence before making broader financial claims. A useful design remedy can be accepted for reliability or clarity without inventing a savings forecast.'),
('Should I time every cleaner?','Define the question with the local team and observe the process transparently. Focus on layout, supplies, product care and dependencies. The purpose is to improve the property’s operating setup, not to create an unsupported ranking of people from incomparable stays.'),
('When should I buy a replacement product?','After the observation identifies a product-related issue and the proposed remedy has been compared with layout, storage or instruction changes. Include the full cost, care process and downstream effects. Preserve purchase records for the separate financial review.')],
'bathroom-refresh-design-brief':[
('Can new finishes solve recurring dampness?','A decorating change does not establish that the source is resolved. Record the observed condition and obtain appropriate assessment. EPA’s moisture guidance provides background, while the responsible professionals determine the property-specific investigation and remedy.'),
('Can I keep the rental open during work?','That decision depends on the actual available rooms, functions, technical requirements and operating obligations. The manager needs the exact affected scope and readiness evidence. Do not assume guests can use an incomplete bathroom or that installation automatically means ready for use.'),
('Should the designer assign renovation costs to tax categories?','The designer should preserve useful descriptions, invoices, payments, changes and photographs. The tax preparer and relevant study provider identify additional evidence and appropriate treatment. Do not manufacture allocations or treat the word refresh as a financial conclusion.')],
'wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan':[
('Is a storage bench enough for a group entry?','Test it with people present and its lid, drawers and nearby doors in use. The right answer depends on the actual arrival items and room. A bench may provide seating while failing as accessible storage during a busy arrival.'),
('Can one location handle storage and drying?','Sometimes, if its product suitability, space, care and operating process support both functions. Record what happens to wet items, how the space is reset and which seasonal conditions change availability. Avoid letting the plan consume the principal guest route.'),
('How should I choose an entry mat or floor protection?','Use the exact product and flooring instructions, the anticipated exposure and the local cleaning process. Test fit and behavior in the installed setting. A material label alone does not prove compatibility or resolve an underlying moisture concern.')],
'portfolio-furnishing-standard-with-local-variation':[
('Should every property use identical furniture?','Standardize the records and required functions first. Exact products still need to fit the local layout, delivery route, exposure and guest brief. Use a documented exception when the standard does not work, and preserve the installed model in that property’s inventory.'),
('Can spare parts be pooled across properties?','Only when exact compatibility, location and access are known. Record revisions and transfers, and remove obsolete assumptions. Visually similar cushions, hardware or covers may not fit the same product, so a shared photograph is not enough.'),
('Does portfolio purchasing eliminate property-level financial records?','No. Keep purchases, assignments, transfers, replacements and credits traceable to the relevant property and evidence. The accountant determines reporting, and a study provider may require separate reconciliations. A central purchasing process should improve those records rather than blur them.')],
'furnishing-project-critical-path':[
('Is a published shipping estimate an installation date?','No. Dispatch, receiving, inspection, movement to the property, installation and final checks are separate milestones. Record the evidence and owner for each. Do not let an estimate become a firm completion assumption simply because it appears repeatedly in the schedule.'),
('How much schedule contingency should I add?','Base it on the actual uncertainties, dependencies, vendor information and exception options. This guide does not prescribe a universal allowance. State the assumptions and review the chain when important information changes instead of hiding uncertainty in a single promised date.'),
('Does the launch schedule establish placed-in-service timing?','It records planned and actual project events, which should be preserved with supporting documents. The tax preparer determines the relevant treatment from the facts and applicable rules. An order, delivery or installation label is not by itself the conclusion.')],
}
for g in LESSONS:
 g['lab']=LABS[g['slug']]
 g['faqs']=FAQS[g['slug']]
assert len(LESSONS)==12
FOLLOWUP={
'large-group-activity-zoning':'''Keep a short configuration register for the group spaces. Give each accepted layout a name and photograph the table, seating and principal route in use. Record which group activities it supports and which items must remain in their assigned locations. When the furniture changes, compare the new dimensions and functions with this record rather than judging the replacement on appearance alone. Ask the local team whether the accepted configuration is practical to restore after each stay. If it requires repeated heavy movement, review the design again. The register should support everyday operation and accurate capacity information, not exist only as a presentation drawing.''',
'studio-rental-zoning-without-extra-furniture':'''Keep the room's expanded configurations beside its product inventory. Record the exact mechanism instructions and the places where displaced cushions, bedding and table leaves are stored. After a replacement, repeat the entire change from daytime to sleeping use. A new model can have a different sweep or opening depth even when the closed dimensions are similar. Ask an unfamiliar local reviewer to follow the setup instructions and record points of confusion. Revise the instructions or arrangement before accepting the change. The studio standard is the whole usable sequence, including where possessions go, rather than a list of individually compact objects.''',
'multigenerational-stay-design-brief':'''Create a question register from real inquiries about the property. Record the question, the verified answer, the evidence and whether the published information needs improvement. Keep personal assumptions about the guest out of the feature record. A question about stairs should lead to accurate stair and room information, not a guess about whether the guest can manage them. Review the record after changes to sleeping arrangements, bathrooms or common rooms. Give the listing owner responsibility for updates so the accepted design brief and the current guest description stay connected. Clear information remains valuable even when the property cannot offer every requested feature.''',
'pet-friendly-furnishing-system':'''Maintain a component history for frequently serviced items. Record the installed model, cover or part identifier, approved care event, observed condition and remedy. When a supplier changes a fabric or construction, obtain the new instructions rather than reusing the previous approval automatically. Review whether a compatible spare was actually useful and whether its storage remained appropriate. Remove obsolete parts from the active register and reconcile new purchases to the property inventory. This history helps distinguish a recurring specification issue from a one-time incident. It also gives the owner useful facts for later purchasing and accounting discussions without assigning a cause or treatment prematurely.''',
'kitchen-inventory-group-meal-test':'''Use the accepted cabinet map as a versioned operating document. Record the installed appliance models and the everyday tools required for the tested meal scenario. If an appliance is replaced, check cookware compatibility and the preparation route again. If a table changes, repeat seating and serving rather than preserving the old capacity statement. Give the local team a place to record essential missing items and repeated storage confusion. Review that evidence before adding more products. A kitchen can become harder to use when every request results in another object without a corresponding storage or care decision. Keep the whole meal sequence as the reference.''',
'rental-bedroom-nighttime-commissioning':'''Keep a bedroom test card with the installed product revisions and accepted arrangement. Mark the date of the nighttime review and any morning-light observation. When the room is reset, compare the controls, luggage location and principal route with the accepted photographs. A cleaner should have an exception process for a broken lamp, missing spare or moved item rather than improvising a different setup silently. Reopen the relevant test when a replacement changes dimensions, compatibility or operation. The record should make a specific room repeatable while allowing later evidence to improve it. It is not a promise that every guest will have the same sleep experience.''',
'rental-accessibility-feature-evidence':'''Assign a record owner for each feature that appears in the listing. Keep the current statement, supporting photograph, measurement details and professional correspondence together. When a proposed purchase could affect the feature, flag that dependency before approval. The reviewer can then decide what new evidence or qualified interpretation is needed. Preserve superseded photographs with their dates, while making the current version obvious to the listing manager. Do not let a convenient old image override the installed condition. A feature record should remain responsive to real changes and questions, and should identify uncertainty without turning it into a broad assurance about the whole property.''',
 'turnover-time-design-study':'''Create a small observation history rather than a permanent performance score. Keep the task definition and context with each result so later comparisons remain understandable. Record when a remedy was adopted and what instructions changed. Ask the team whether the change remains practical after ordinary use and whether it created another storage or care problem. If the product or layout changes again, the earlier result may no longer apply. Use the history to choose further investigation and purchasing priorities. Preserve actual costs separately from hypothetical savings so the operating review does not become an unsupported financial forecast or a claim that one intervention caused every later improvement.''',
'bathroom-refresh-design-brief':'''At closeout, compare the final room against the approved scope and each technical provider's completion record. Keep the remaining punch list visible, including who can resolve it and what evidence will close it. Save the installed product information and permitted care methods where the operating team can find them. Reconcile vendor changes and credits rather than leaving the original estimate as the only financial record. If a later moisture or function issue appears, connect it to the condition history and obtain appropriate review. The refresh record should show what was observed, assessed and installed, without claiming that an attractive finished photograph proves every underlying issue was resolved.''',
'wet-gear-entry-and-storage-plan':'''Maintain an arrival reset card for ordinary and seasonal conditions. Show where guests can set down bags, where equipment belongs and which drying locations are available. Photograph doors and storage in their usable arrangement, and identify any owner-only area clearly. Ask the local team to record recurring misplaced items or damp conditions. Review whether the cause is an unclear location, unsuitable product or a building concern requiring specialist attention. When equipment or guest capacity changes, repeat the occupied-arrival test. The entry should remain coherent during real use, including the exception when weather makes an outdoor location unavailable, rather than depending on a staged photograph of an empty room.''',
'portfolio-furnishing-standard-with-local-variation':'''Give the portfolio standard a revision number and an effective date. List which properties use that revision and which approved exceptions remain active. When a central product is discontinued, evaluate the proposed successor against actual installed dimensions, care and parts compatibility before updating the standard. Tell local teams which records change and which existing items can remain. Keep stock transfers and replacement purchases traceable by property. Review whether standardization reduces ambiguity in the operating records rather than simply increasing bulk orders. A successful standard supports local decisions and financial reconciliation; it should not erase the evidence that makes each property's installed setup and subsequent changes understandable.''',
'furnishing-project-critical-path':'''At completion, compare planned and actual milestones without rewriting the earlier assumptions as if they had been certain. Record which decisions, vendor events or site conditions changed the sequence. Use that evidence to prepare the next project, retaining differences in location, scope and team. A previous project's duration is a reference point, not a guaranteed schedule for another property. Keep the final chronology linked to receiving checks, installation records and readiness approval. The operating and financial reviewers should be able to distinguish when goods were ordered, received, installed and available for their intended use, and identify where additional evidence or professional interpretation is still required.''',
}
for g in LESSONS:g['sections'].append(('Keep the accepted result useful after the project',FOLLOWUP[g['slug']]))
